<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?> <?xml-stylesheet type="text/xsl" href="rss.xsl"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"> <channel> <title>DOX</title><description>News and releases of Dox, the web backend framework for Dart.</description><link>https://dartondox.dev/blog/</link><atom:link href="https://dartondox.dev/blog/feed_rss_created.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml" /> <docs>https://github.com/dartondox/dox</docs><language>en</language> <pubDate>Wed, 25 Nov 2026 11:55:39 -0000</pubDate> <lastBuildDate>Wed, 25 Nov 2026 11:55:39 -0000</lastBuildDate> <ttl>1440</ttl> <generator>MkDocs RSS plugin - v1.19.0</generator> <image> <url>https://dartondox.dev/blog/assets/social-card.png</url> <title>DOX</title> <link>https://dartondox.dev/blog/</link> </image> <item> <title>Security fixes release: 25 November 2026</title> <author>Dox team</author> <category>Releases</category> <category>release</category> <category>security</category> <description>This release has a security fix for the native HTTP server and for the native HTTP client. We recommend that you update. The server also has 10 more fixes...</description> <link>https://dartondox.dev/blog/dox-patch-release-2026-11-25/</link> <pubDate>Wed, 25 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/feed_rss_created.xml">DOX</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://dartondox.dev/blog/dox-patch-release-2026-11-25/</guid> <enclosure url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/assets/images/social/dox-patch-release-2026-11-25.png" type="image/png" length="120644" /> </item> <item> <title>Dox patch release: 24 November 2026</title> <author>Dox team</author> <category>Releases</category> <category>release</category> <description>This release is patch versions only. Dox has a new native HTTP client, and five packages now send their requests through it. The Linux libraries of the...</description> <link>https://dartondox.dev/blog/dox-patch-release-2026-11-24/</link> <pubDate>Tue, 24 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/feed_rss_created.xml">DOX</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://dartondox.dev/blog/dox-patch-release-2026-11-24/</guid> <enclosure url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/assets/images/social/dox-patch-release-2026-11-24.png" type="image/png" length="117303" /> </item> <item> <title>Dox patch release: 22 November 2026</title> <author>Dox team</author> <category>Releases</category> <category>release</category> <description>This release is patch versions only. It makes the tail latency lower on servers with 4 or more CPU cores, and it makes routes with params faster. One fix...</description> <link>https://dartondox.dev/blog/dox-patch-release-2026-11-22/</link> <pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/feed_rss_created.xml">DOX</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://dartondox.dev/blog/dox-patch-release-2026-11-22/</guid> <enclosure url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/assets/images/social/dox-patch-release-2026-11-22.png" type="image/png" length="117373" /> </item> <item> <title>Dox patch release: 21 November 2026</title> <author>Dox team</author> <category>Releases</category> <category>release</category> <description>This release is patch versions only. It adds the MCP protocol 2026-07-28, new route options in dox_core, and fixes in auth, ORM, validation, mail, Socket.IO...</description> <link>https://dartondox.dev/blog/dox-patch-release-2026-11-21/</link> <pubDate>Sat, 21 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/feed_rss_created.xml">DOX</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://dartondox.dev/blog/dox-patch-release-2026-11-21/</guid> <enclosure url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/assets/images/social/dox-patch-release-2026-11-21.png" type="image/png" length="117033" /> </item> <item> <title>GraphQL in Dox</title> <author>Dox team</author> <category>Releases</category> <category>graphql</category> <category>release</category> <description>The new dox_graphql package makes a GraphQL API for your app from your models. You add annotations to a model, run the builder, and the API is ready.</description> <link>https://dartondox.dev/blog/graphql/</link> <pubDate>Sat, 21 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/feed_rss_created.xml">DOX</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://dartondox.dev/blog/graphql/</guid> <enclosure url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/assets/images/social/graphql.png" type="image/png" length="95030" /> </item> <item> <title>HTTP/2 and gRPC in Dox</title> <author>Dox team</author> <category>Releases</category> <category>grpc</category> <category>http2</category> <category>release</category> <description>Dox now serves HTTP/2 and gRPC on the same port as your HTTP routes. HTTP/2 is off by default, so an app that does not turn it on works the same as before.</description> <link>https://dartondox.dev/blog/http2-and-grpc/</link> <pubDate>Sat, 21 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/feed_rss_created.xml">DOX</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://dartondox.dev/blog/http2-and-grpc/</guid> <enclosure url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/assets/images/social/http2-and-grpc.png" type="image/png" length="100087" /> </item> <item> <title>Dox vs Serverpod: which Dart backend fits your app</title> <author>Dox team</author> <category>Guides</category> <category>comparison</category> <description>Dox and Serverpod side by side: endpoints, models, ORM, migrations, background jobs, auth, mail and deploy, with code from each, so you can pick a Dart backend.</description> <link>https://dartondox.dev/blog/dox-vs-serverpod/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/feed_rss_created.xml">DOX</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://dartondox.dev/blog/dox-vs-serverpod/</guid> <enclosure url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/assets/images/social/dox-vs-serverpod.png" type="image/png" length="124705" /> </item> <item> <title>Dart backend frameworks: how to choose</title> <author>Dox team</author> <category>Guides</category> <category>comparison</category> <category>getting started</category> <description>Serverpod, Dart Frog, Shelf, Vania and Dox compared by what they include: ORM, migrations, auth, queues, mail and deploy, so you can pick a Dart backend.</description> <link>https://dartondox.dev/blog/dart-backend-frameworks-how-to-choose/</link> <pubDate>Tue, 17 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/feed_rss_created.xml">DOX</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://dartondox.dev/blog/dart-backend-frameworks-how-to-choose/</guid> <enclosure url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/assets/images/social/dart-backend-frameworks-how-to-choose.png" type="image/png" length="119162" /> </item> <item> <title>Dox 3.2 patches: faster, less memory, fewer leaks</title> <author>Dox team</author> <category>Releases</category> <category>release</category> <description>This release is patch versions only. It makes Dox faster, uses less memory, and fixes memory leaks in long-running apps. There are no API changes. A few...</description> <link>https://dartondox.dev/blog/dox-3-2-patches/</link> <pubDate>Tue, 17 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/feed_rss_created.xml">DOX</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://dartondox.dev/blog/dox-3-2-patches/</guid> <enclosure url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/assets/images/social/dox-3-2-patches.png" type="image/png" length="123364" /> </item> <item> <title>Dox 3.2 is out</title> <author>Dox team</author> <category>Releases</category> <category>release</category> <description>Dox 3.2 adds dox add to set up a package with one command, read replicas, streaming queries, model dirty tracking, job batches and chains, a database cache...</description> <link>https://dartondox.dev/blog/dox-3-2/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 13 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/feed_rss_created.xml">DOX</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://dartondox.dev/blog/dox-3-2/</guid> <enclosure url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/assets/images/social/dox-3-2.png" type="image/png" length="94794" /> </item> <item> <title>Background jobs and scheduled tasks in Dox</title> <author>Dox team</author> <category>Guides</category> <category>mail</category> <category>queue</category> <category>scheduler</category> <category>tutorial</category> <description>This post shows how to move slow work out of the request with a queue, and how to run work on a timer with the scheduler.</description> <link>https://dartondox.dev/blog/background-jobs-and-scheduled-tasks/</link> <pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/feed_rss_created.xml">DOX</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://dartondox.dev/blog/background-jobs-and-scheduled-tasks/</guid> <enclosure url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/assets/images/social/background-jobs-and-scheduled-tasks.png" type="image/png" length="120907" /> </item> <item> <title>A full-stack app with Dox and Inertia</title> <author>Dox team</author> <category>Guides</category> <category>full stack</category> <category>inertia</category> <category>react</category> <category>tutorial</category> <description>This post shows how to build a React app on top of Dox with Inertia, with no separate API between them.</description> <link>https://dartondox.dev/blog/full-stack-app-with-inertia/</link> <pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/feed_rss_created.xml">DOX</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://dartondox.dev/blog/full-stack-app-with-inertia/</guid> <enclosure url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/assets/images/social/full-stack-app-with-inertia.png" type="image/png" length="109863" /> </item> <item> <title>Dox vs Go: speed and memory</title> <author>Dox team</author> <category>Performance</category> <category>benchmark</category> <category>go</category> <category>memory</category> <category>performance</category> <description>In our published benchmark on 2 CPU cores, a Dox app handled 2× to 4.8× the requests per second of a Go app. Go used less memory once a database was added.</description> <link>https://dartondox.dev/blog/dox-vs-go-speed-and-memory/</link> <pubDate>Tue, 10 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/feed_rss_created.xml">DOX</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://dartondox.dev/blog/dox-vs-go-speed-and-memory/</guid> <enclosure url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/assets/images/social/dox-vs-go-speed-and-memory.png" type="image/png" length="110388" /> </item> <item> <title>Build a Dart API with Dox</title> <author>Dox team</author> <category>Guides</category> <category>getting started</category> <category>tutorial</category> <description>This post shows how to go from an empty folder to a tested API with a database, validation and a native binary, in a few short steps.</description> <link>https://dartondox.dev/blog/build-a-dart-api-with-dox/</link> <pubDate>Mon, 09 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/feed_rss_created.xml">DOX</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://dartondox.dev/blog/build-a-dart-api-with-dox/</guid> <enclosure url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/assets/images/social/build-a-dart-api-with-dox.png" type="image/png" length="98773" /> </item> <item> <title>A tour of Dox features</title> <author>Dox team</author> <category>Introduction</category> <category>features</category> <category>packages</category> <description>This post is a short tour of the parts that come with Dox, from routing and validation to the ORM, login, queues, mail and the CLI.</description> <link>https://dartondox.dev/blog/dox-feature-tour/</link> <pubDate>Mon, 09 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/feed_rss_created.xml">DOX</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://dartondox.dev/blog/dox-feature-tour/</guid> <enclosure url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/assets/images/social/dox-feature-tour.png" type="image/png" length="99732" /> </item> <item> <title>Dox vs Node.js: memory and speed</title> <author>Dox team</author> <category>Performance</category> <category>benchmark</category> <category>memory</category> <category>node</category> <category>performance</category> <description>In our published benchmark on 2 CPU cores, a Dox app served more requests per second than a Node.js app and used much less memory.</description> <link>https://dartondox.dev/blog/dox-vs-nodejs-memory-and-speed/</link> <pubDate>Mon, 09 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/feed_rss_created.xml">DOX</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://dartondox.dev/blog/dox-vs-nodejs-memory-and-speed/</guid> <enclosure url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/assets/images/social/dox-vs-nodejs-memory-and-speed.png" type="image/png" length="113387" /> </item> <item> <title>Live updates with Socket.IO in Dox</title> <author>Dox team</author> <category>Guides</category> <category>realtime</category> <category>socket.io</category> <category>tutorial</category> <description>This post shows how to add live updates to a Dox app with Socket.IO. When one user changes data, the screens of the other users change too, with no refresh.</description> <link>https://dartondox.dev/blog/live-updates-with-socketio/</link> <pubDate>Mon, 09 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/feed_rss_created.xml">DOX</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://dartondox.dev/blog/live-updates-with-socketio/</guid> <enclosure url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/assets/images/social/live-updates-with-socketio.png" type="image/png" length="111518" /> </item> <item> <title>What makes Dox fast</title> <author>Dox team</author> <category>Performance</category> <category>architecture</category> <category>performance</category> <description>Dox is fast because your Dart code runs as a compiled binary, the network and database work happens in native code on the same thread, and most of the setup...</description> <link>https://dartondox.dev/blog/what-makes-dox-fast/</link> <pubDate>Mon, 09 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/feed_rss_created.xml">DOX</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://dartondox.dev/blog/what-makes-dox-fast/</guid> <enclosure url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/assets/images/social/what-makes-dox-fast.png" type="image/png" length="100886" /> </item> <item> <title>Dox 3.1 is out</title> <author>Dox team</author> <category>Releases</category> <category>release</category> <description>Dox 3.1 adds new routing tools, request helpers, new ORM features, access tokens, a new MCP package, mail and queue features, and many security and bug fixes.</description> <link>https://dartondox.dev/blog/dox-3-1/</link> <pubDate>Sun, 08 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/feed_rss_created.xml">DOX</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://dartondox.dev/blog/dox-3-1/</guid> <enclosure url="https://dartondox.dev/blog/assets/images/social/dox-3-1.png" type="image/png" length="93534" /> </item> </channel> </rss>